{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/153540-kismet-s-oneiromancy-alterations","id":153540,"title":"Kismet's Oneiromancy Alterations","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-29T22:25:33-07:00","id":153540,"title":"Kismet's Oneiromancy Alterations","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">I've been working on revamping many parts of the dream rules based on how our group plays and some basic guiding principles. There are some aspects I haven't changed at all, such as the Intensity bonuses for the different types of dreams. There are some supplementary rules from later books that we'll be adding, like the special rules for mages and dream Intensity. I'll endeavor to explain the rationale behind the things I've changed or added, which become more obvious and numerous later on.<br /> <br /> \u200bI'm interested in constructive feedback, but since we've already been playtesting some of this, I'm posting this for discussion and some outside opinion rather than trying to convince anyone else to use it. If something is faulty or there's a possibility I haven't foreseen, I'll be glad to think it over and make further alterations.<br /> <br /> This is all drawn from a giant dreaming reference I'm making for the use of my group, so there are abbreviations and such. I hope you enjoy it. =)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#000000\">Changelings do not need to be in the Hedge or a Hollow to enter dreams, nor do they suffer from seeming curses on dream rolls of any kind.</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#000000\">We've never restricted dreamwork to the Hedge, since our group does a LOT of dreamwork no matter where they are.<br /> Further, it seemed like a fun idea to me that changelings get to be what they imagine themselves to be in dreams, and that it's the one place they can leave their curses behind.</span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Intensity of Dream:</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> At the onset, roll Wits + Resolve + Supernatural Advantage + miscellaneous bonuses \u2013 penalties for loud noises and other distractions nearby.</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#000000\">We've reasoned that supernatural creatures generally have more intense dreams (which provides them a modicum of basic protection).</span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Vampires</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">: While in torpor, vampires who have memory dreams roll Resolve + Composure with a penalty equal to 10 \u2013 Morality. If the rolls, the dream is a twisted version of the memory. On a dramatic failure, the altered memory supplants the true one.</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Werewolves</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">: Gibbous Moon auspice werewolves can induce prophetic dreams in themselves; add their Renown to the Intensity.</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Mages</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">: Mages can choose to add or subtract Gnosis from the Intensity of their own dreams.</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#000000\">Since we've started to get tangled in dreams of vampires and such, I want to add these rules from the book I found them in.</span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Miscellaneous bonuses to Intensity</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">: Ambien (+1 die), B complex vitamins (+1 die), derangement or Condition (+1 die), hallucinogens (+2 dice), meditation (+2 dice).</span> </div> </div> </div>Because yeah, some things at least seem to affect how vividly you dream, so we've added bonuses for them. I'm also planning to take penalties into account more often due to discomfort and environmental factors. Many things can keep you from falling under deeply.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Entering your Own Dreams</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">: No roll or expenditure needed; can do so at will.</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Entering your Fetch\u2019s Dream: </b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">1 Willpower; no roll for entry; Storyteller rolls changeling's Presence + Wyrd each time changeling invades their fetch's dreams. Target number is the fetch's WP; once equalled or exceeded, fetch can learn to spend WP to haunt their changeling.</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Entering a Dream Pledge:</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> 1 Glamour; no roll for entry.</span> </div> </div> </div>We found the rolling system to enter dreams tedious from the start. Even though we understand that it's supposed to reflect the difficulty of crossing into a dreamscape and how much time it takes to get there, over time it made less sense for us. If a dream is going to get interrupted, it's going to get interrupted, and that's ST fiat. If it takes too long to get into and the dreamer wakes up, again, ST fiat. But we figure, if you have an inherent connection or one forged by a pledge, you pay the cost and you're in.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T22:37:50-07:00","id":153554,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:arial\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dreamscaping</span></b><span style=\"color:#000000\"> involves interacting with a dream within a sleeper\u2019s mind and consists of three basic categories of maneuvers: analysis, manipulation, and healing. Unless specified otherwise, an oneiromancer can only focus on one maneuver per night, and a dreamer can only benefit or suffer from one dreamscaping ability per night. An oneiromancer can engage the dreamer directly and socially without using a special maneuver, but no extra benefit will come out of the interaction (though the dreamer is quite likely to remember much of what was said).</span> </div> </div> </div>The first major move I'm making is to restructure what dreamscaping is and how it is divided up. There seemed to be natural divisions suggested in the types of maneuvers presented in the core book. We've also had characters speak to dreamers directly without really changing anything, which has regularly borne interesting fruits, so we wanted the rules to reflect that.</span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Oneiromancers who are very successful in their work are liable to leave deep impressions on the dreamer. A low-Intensity dream can be overwritten by something much more powerful, but the dreamer is likely to recall the details upon waking and with an exceptional success, the dreamer might even have an idea of who\u2019s responsible, whether they saw the oneiromancer or not (see specific bonuses and penalties below).</span></span><br /> </div> </div> </div>While we understand why dreams might come apart from being messed with by an outside party, we did not enjoy how easy it was to tear the dream apart and have to spend Willpower to keep it together. It seemed to penalize changelings who are good at dreamwork, which got frustrating all around. My players wanted their characters to be good at it without having to hold back all the time, and I didn't blame them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">While changelings can analyze, perform basic alterations, and scour the intensity of their own dreams, they cannot perform any other dream maneuvers for themselves, so changelings do it for each other.</span></span> </div> </div> </div>This remains the same, and motleys retain their value for helping each other in these ways.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Changelings who dreamscape while sleeping in the Hedge or in a Hollow gain a +1 bonus. If the target dreamer is also sleeping in the Hedge or a Hollow, the bonus is +2, total.</span></span> </div> </div> </div>Dreamscaping might not be restricted to the Hedge in these rules, but being in that realm helps oneiromancy all around. The bonus applies to all dreamscaping maneuvers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 05-30-2014, 01:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T22:48:49-07:00","id":153557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Dream analysis</b> maneuvers do not change aspects of the ongoing dream in any appreciable way. They are observational, and while an oneiromancer might be able to interact with the dreamer to a limited extent, too much interference will get in the way of being able to read the dream as it stands. </div> </div> </div>This is the first new major category of dreamscaping, which gathers together the investigative maneuvers. Accordingly, the rolls are based on Intelligence rather than Wits, as analytic maneuvers tend to be based in Intelligence in the nWoD system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Analyze Dream</b>: Intelligence + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity to recognize the type of dream. Storyteller rolls the Intensity and adds any successes to the player's results if the oneiromancer is analyzing a prophetic dream. </div> </div> </div>The last part is something we'll be trying out, going with the idea that true prophetic dreams stand out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Learn about the Dreamer</b>: Intelligence + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. Each roll takes all night. Normally, every five successes allows you to learn one aspect of your choice: Virtue, Vice, permanent Willlpower rating, derangements or Conditions (one per 5 successes), Merits (Mental and Social only), Flaws (Mental and Social only), or any mental or emotional supernatural power used on dreamer within the last month. However, it only takes three successes to learn the following information: Virtue and Vice from wish fulfillment dreams; permanent Willpower rating from memory dreams; derangements or Conditions from nightmares; Merits and Flaws from recurring memory dreams; and supernatural powers used on the dreamer from recurring dreams. </div> </div> </div>I wanted to integrate the different types of dreams further and this seemed like an ideal way to do that without overbalancing anything. It should also give motleys ways to learn about each other more easily.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Search for Dream Poison</b>: Wits + Empathy or Occult + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. Each roll takes all night and requires a number of total successes equal to the Intensity of the contagion-dream. Oneiromancers can use this technique to search for other types of intruders in dreams, making the same roll vs. the intruder\u2019s Wits or Manipulation + Stealth or Subterfuge + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity roll to hide or blend into the scene. Attacking an intruder leads to dream battle (see below) and is not handled through standard dreamscaping maneuvers. </div> </div> </div>Since there are dream creatures, other changelings, and other such intruders that can venture into one's dreams, it seemed like a good idea to determine a way to look for them. Also, later on I add some complications which mean that more than one changeling can be in someone's dream at a time, so there's more to keep an eye out for in one's dreams.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 06-02-2014, 03:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-30T09:11:14-07:00","id":153920,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Dream manipulation</b>: The second category of dreamscaping involves direct manipulation of a dream\u2019s content, whether or not it is for the dreamer\u2019s good. The oneiromancer will rewrite and overcome the natural plot and look of an ongoing dream (though oneiromancers often try to use the symbols they find to make their work seem less obvious or distressing). </div> </div> </div>The main change to these maneuvers involves the rolls required. General alterations remain the standard Wits + Empathy + Wyrd, but social manipulation reverts to Manipulation, and teaching becomes an Intelligence action. Siphoning is added here because changelings so often twist dreams so they can get the most Glamour out of them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Alter Dream</b>: Wits + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity to make general changes. Gain 9 again when recreating elements from memory. These alterations can be impressive, but they are purely cosmetic and do not grant the creator or dreamer any bonuses or penalties. You can make general alterations and perform one other dream maneuver in the same night. </div> </div> </div>The PCs in my games often recreate things they know so well from the waking world that it makes sense they'd have an easier time of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Convince the Dreamer</b>: Manipulation + Persuasion + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity to implant one subliminal message in the dreamer\u2019s mind. When he encounters a trigger in real life he subconsciously reacts to it. Every success adds 1 die to Manipulation rolls when using that trigger on the dreamer in the waking world. You decide how many dice to use at a time, till they are used up or a week passes. Each dreamer can only have one subliminal message at a time, and each one lasts a week. </div> </div> </div>Didn't need to change this one at all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Nightmares</b>: Manipulation + Intimidation + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. The oneiromancer can give such horrible nightmares to the dreamer that, if the result is equal or greater than the dreamer's permanent Willpower, they won't regain any Willpower from resting that evening. If the oneiromancer gains an exceptional success on a nightmare, they will suffer a -1 social penalty with the dreamer for 24 hours afterwards, as the dreamer is beset with a vague uneasiness or the sense that the character means them harm. </div> </div> </div>The last part has to do with the mention above that doing an exceptional job on dreamwork can catch the dreamer's attention (whether they understand why or not). Just because you didn't tear the dream apart with your success doesn't mean there won't be some consequences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Siphoning Glamour</b>: Composure + Wits + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity to harvest from a dream that has been unchanged, or the oneiromancer makes the appropriate roll (as determined by the type of action) + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity to change the dream as they see fit. Each success is a point of Glamour gained by the oneiromancer. </div> </div> </div>Didn't need to change this one at all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Sleep Teaching</b>: Intelligence + appropriate mental or social Skill + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. The teacher must possess the Skill, Merit (Storyteller discretion), or Contract in question and cannot teach above their own ability level. The dreamer dreams about practicing skills in his sleep. One night of Dream Teaching is equivalent to a day of study. A critical success grants the dreamer a free experience point (or Beat) that can only be spent on the Skill, Merit, or Contract being taught. Can only have one point or Beat in storage per skill; once it is raised, another can be taught. </div> </div> </div>This just needed a bit more clarification regarding what the teacher can teach.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 06-02-2014, 03:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-30T09:41:53-07:00","id":153947,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Dream healing</b> maneuvers are entirely beneficial for the dreamer, whether they realize it consciously or not. If an oneiromancer does exceptionally well with a healing maneuver, they will gain a +1 bonus to social interactions with the dreamer for the next 24 hours as the dreamer has the sense that the character means them well. </div> </div> </div>This is the positive counterpart to making nightmares that are too good; if you help someone in their dreams, it'll last a little while after in their attitude toward you. As with the last category, basic rolls have largely been changed. While I see the neatness in making everything based on Wits + Empathy, it's never felt right or made much sense to me. All changelings can engage in dreamscaping but not all of them will be good at every type of maneuver.<br /> <br /> It also seemed like being able to calm and heal others would come from being able to remain calm yourself, while scouring and stress relief would rely more on your resolve to see it done.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Healing Sleep</b>: Intelligence + Wits or Medicine + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. The dreamer is put into an unusually deep restful sleep that allows the body to heal at a faster rate. Each night of Healing Sleep is equivalent to a day of rest; an exceptional success equals 2. </div> </div> </div>While healing someone in their dreams can be based on just a raw desire to help (Wits), there's no reason someone with real medical experience couldn't use it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Psychotherapy</b>: Manipulation + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. Each roll takes all night. Each night equals a week of normal psychotherapy. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Scour the Intensity</b>: Wits + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. Done to reduce nightmares or get rid of dream poison. Each success lowers the Intensity of the dream by one. If the Intensity is reduced below 1, the dream ends. An oneiromancer can keep the dream together by spending a Willpower point, but it will remain at low Intensity and no other maneuvers will be possible. To scour dream poison, the roll has no penalty, but is opposed by a roll of the Intensity of dream-poison. Each success above the poison reduces its Intensity by 1. If the contagion wins, the oneiromancer takes 1 point of Willpower damage per success. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Stress Relief</b>: Manipulation + Subterfuge or Persuasion + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. If the dreamer\u2019s Virtue and Vice are known, the oneiromancer can create a dream that will bolster the dreamer's sense of self. Normal success allows the dreamer to recover a point of Willpower as if he'd fulfilled a Vice. An exceptional success allows recovery of all Willpower as if fulfilling a Virtue. </div> </div> </div>This is going with the idea that you're either trying to trick the mind into feeling stronger, or you're trying to persuade the person that they are awesome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 06-02-2014, 03:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-30T09:59:42-07:00","id":153958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Dreamweaving </b>is the art of putting a dream inside of an object. Anyone who sleeps in contact with the dream vessel will experience the dream and its effect. Healing sleep, Stress Relief, Sleep Teaching, and Nightmares can be put into a dream vessel, as well as the powers listed below. It is rumored that certain Entitlements teach techniques to install other powers in dream vessels, as well. All creatures who dream can benefit from a dream vessel, not just beings who are naturally bound up in the Wyrd (thus, usability extends beyond changelings, fetches, and pledgebound mortals). </div> </div> </div>In our games, if you sleep with a dream vessel and can dream, it has a chance to activate for you. You don't have to be fae in any sense of the word, which is part of why dream vessels are so very valuable. I wanted to have that spelled out for the future because as the group meets different types of creatures, no doubt it will come up. In the book, frustratingly, one sentence says that &quot;any dreamer may experience the dream,&quot; but the very next sentence restricts it to &quot;any changeling, fetch or human bound in a dreaming-tasked pledge.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To make a dream vessel, first you must find an object that is associated with the type of dream bound within it. Then, spend 1 Glamour and make an extended Intelligence + Empathy + Wyrd roll to determine the Intensity of the dream. Each success represents half an hour of effort, and only one roll can be made per day. Then, roll for whatever effects you wish the dream to have. Only one effect roll is allowed per vessel. If the dreamer doesn't know the vessel is the source of the dreams, they resist reflexively with Resolve + Composure + Supernatural Advantage. </div> </div> </div>My group has asked for clarification on how long it actually takes to weave a dream vessel. Sure, you only get one roll per day, but how many minutes or hours in that day are filled with that one task? Changelings in my games tend to be quite busy and they don't engage in day-long tasks lightly. We've seen no reason that weaving a dream vessel should be the only thing you get to do that day.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Wyrd Dream</b>: A Wyrd dream allows the use of Contracts (other than Dream) on the dreamer through a dream vessel. Only Contracts that can target people other than the user can be used. After the Intensity of the dream has been determined, the normal roll for the Contract is used. If that roll is too weak, let the Glamour in the vessel fade and try again at a -1 penalty. Either the Contract activation roll or the Intensity of the dream will affect the dreamer, whichever is lowest. If a resistance roll is allowed, those successes are subtracted from the Contract\u2019s activation as normal. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Personalize Dream</b>: If the dreamer is very well-known, through having learned at least two traits about the dreamer through dream analysis, a dream vessel can be made specifically for them. The final Intensity is increased by the oneiromancer's dots in Empathy or Wyrd, whichever is greater \u2013 but only for that dreamer. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Analyze Dream Vessel</b>: If a changeling suspects that an object is a dream vessel, she can analyze the object. An extended Wits + Composure + Wyrd roll is required, difficulty of the dream vessel\u2019s Intensity. Each roll takes 10 minutes, and a changeling can only roll as many times as their Wyrd rating per dream vessel. This reveals whether or not an object is a dream vessel, but it does not reveal the type of dream stored inside. </div> </div> </div>I added a stipulation on how many attempts you get to determine if an item is a dream vessel, based on how strong you are with the Wyrd. It feels right.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 05-30-2014, 01:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-30T10:16:22-07:00","id":153965,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Changelings can engage each other, fetches, Incubi, Morpheans, lucid dreamers, or the Fae in <b>dream combat</b>. This violence is outside of any symbolic nightmare and can go on until a winner emerges; while oneiromachy does not count as a dream maneuver like those outlined above, it will interrupt efforts to perform a nightly dream maneuver. Any dreamer that has a battle inside their own dream automatically loses a point of Willpower from the trauma of having such wild Wyrd forces thrown about. </div> </div> </div>I didn't feel the need to change much about dream battle; I just wanted to specify who is capable of actually engaging in proper dream combat, taking into account the creatures of later books. Non-lucid dreamers cannot be engaged in this special way, which requires the ability to respond in kind. They can play the stage for a dream battle between other lucid dreamers but cannot enter into it themselves. Not only does this make dream battle special, but it also prevents some abuses which could come up later on. It's nice to want to show up in a werewolf's dream and start beating the Willpower out of them, but changelings are not (yet) the True Fae and are not capable of that level of abuse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 05-30-2014, 01:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-30T10:37:07-07:00","id":153977,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Group Dreams</b>: The Dreaming task of a pledge can allow for group interactions. As long as each person agrees, the task can allow all of the pledgebound to gather in one member\u2019s dream (provided enough of them are oneiromancers). Further, a group does not have to specify a particular direction for guardianship. The pledge allows each member to guard the dreams of any other person within the pledge, so long as each of the pledgebound receives dream services within the time period everyone agrees to (thus, a motley can agree that everyone will receive at least one dream visit a week by someone else in the motley). </div> </div> </div>This is our major addition to the dreaming rules. Since early on in our time with Changeling, we have allowed group interactions in dreams through the motley pledge. We've also allowed for flexibility in who tends to whom; so long as everyone is serving and getting served, it counts.<br /> <br /> We have also gone on extended group adventures in a dreamscape, and I wanted some part of the rules to reflect this. The most recent example was when the party met in one motley member's dream and were guided along the Skein to the dream of a catatonic child none of them had had the chance to befriend or pledge with. They suspected that one of their Keepers had visited the girl and left her in such a state, and since the doctor's count help her, they had to try. Within her admittedly unique dreamscape, they found the parts of her psyche in disarray after a monster (a True Fae) had swept through and damaged her mental landscape. Her Courage, Memory, Safety, and Hope (all personified) were missing and the changelings went about trying to find them. They didn't use dream maneuvers as above but made general changes (to themselves) to navigate and to convince the aspects of her battered psyche to work with them, come back together, and try again. The one maneuver they finally made was changing the dream to bury the open wound left behind from when the Keeper rended into her dreams to read the Wyrd. They knew they'd gotten somewhere when she came to not long after. This is the kind of fun we've had through this sort of thing, so yes, I want to encourage it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Within pledgebound dreams</b>, a group can act as a team in order to produce a dream maneuver or make general alterations in one member\u2019s dreamscape. This most often applies within a motley, but it can also apply when one person in the group has a pledge to guard someone else\u2019s dreams and needs help to hold up their end of the bargain (as with scouring particularly potent dream poison). The first step is for the group to choose a primary actor (usually the changeling with the most experience in the type of maneuver they want to attempt). The number of assistants allowed on the roll are limited by the primary actor\u2019s Wyrd. Neither a group nor an individual can engage non-lucid dreamers in a dream battle, but if a fae intruder is found, each member of the team can act on their own in dream combat. </div> </div> </div>Having a pledge with someone makes dreamwork easier, so I wanted the rules to reflect that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Outside of pledgebound dreams</b>, a group that is bound to each other can meet in one member\u2019s dream and Skeinwalk together to another destination (provided they have a way to access the Skein). This requires designating one guide to lead everyone along the Skein to the correct dream. A changeling guide can only lead as many others along the Skein as they have dots in Wyrd. Once within the target dream, the group can act as a team to affect the dreamer and/or engage intruders, but any success on a maneuver or battle greater than the Intensity of the dream threatens to unravel it. A Willpower point can be spent to keep it intact, but each member of the team can only spend Willpower once for that purpose. </div> </div> </div>You can get into dreams without a pledge via the Skein but that doesn't mean you belong there. While I got rid of the idea that too many successes will tear apart the dream for pledgebound dreamwork, I brought it back for this situation. Likewise, just because you can take others with you doesn't mean you can lead an army across the Skein into one person's dream space, so I added a limitation based on the guide's Wyrd rating, which seemed appropriate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 06-01-2014, 06:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-30T10:44:11-07:00","id":153991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Other can weave a single use of a Contract into a favored mortal\u2019s dream, investing a point of Glamour to let the mortal hold onto the Contract until using it on a chosen target. The mortal must make a degeneration check when they do so, however, using their Morality rating <i>\u2013</i> the power level of the Contract just used. A mortal at Morality 7 who just unleashed a level 5 Contract would be committing a Morality 2 sin. </div> </div> </div>The only real change I made to the rules for <b>dream warping</b> was here. The book says &quot;The mortal must make a degeneration check as though he'd just committed a sin against a Morality rating of 7 - the Wyrd prerequisite of the Contract just used.&quot; And as many times as I read it, it never made a lick more sense, because Contracts don't have Wyrd prerequisites. So I figure, the more powerful the Contract that's bound up into a mortal, the worse it is for their sanity. It's simple, straightforward, and doesn't make my head ache when trying to read it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":227,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","user":"KismetRose","date":". 05-30-2014, 01:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T14:49:57-07:00","id":155991,"title":"","content":"The dreaming rules are one of my favorite parts of Changeling, so, yay! I'm delighted to see a thread with so much content aimed at improving them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We've never restricted dreamwork to the Hedge, since our group does a LOT of dreamwork no matter where they are. </div> </div> </div>I agree with this move. The restriction doesn't add anything to games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Further, it seemed like a fun idea to me that changelings get to be what they imagine themselves to be in dreams, and that it's the one place they can leave their curses behind. </div> </div> </div>By this do you mean that changelings appear as their former human selves, or that they simply don't suffer their curse's penalties? (So wizened are less surly but still wrinkly-faced, ogres are less gullible but still brutish-looking, etc.)<br /> <br /> I like the notion of dreaming changelings being able to shed their fae miens, which are probably way more upsetting to many Lost than losing the 10-again quality on some rolls. It drives home the dream-world's feel of being a fantasy realm where anything is possible, where people can find escapism/wish fulfillment, etc. I could see more than a few changelings who hate their miens becoming dangerously addicted to time spent dreaming.<br /> <br /> Changelings who've truly accepted their miens as part of themselves, though, I think would appear as they were. I'd probably leave the choice of how their PCs look to players, since it's only cosmetic and temporary to boot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We've reasoned that supernatural creatures generally have more intense dreams (which provides them a modicum of basic protection). </div> </div> </div>And also cuts both ways, since their nightmares will be more likely to cost them Willpower. That fits with the general effects of having high dots in a power stat--extra benefits, but also extra drawbacks. (Though if you don't want that, they could also add their power stat to Willpower when resisting nightmares.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We found the rolling system to enter dreams tedious from the start. Even though we understand that it's supposed to reflect the difficulty of crossing into a dreamscape and how much time it takes to get there, over time it made less sense for us. If a dream is going to get interrupted, it's going to get interrupted, and that's ST fiat. If it takes too long to get into and the dreamer wakes up, again, ST fiat. But we figure, if you have an inherent connection or one forged by a pledge, you pay the cost and you're in. </div> </div> </div>This. If time isn't a factor and the PCs aren't being actively threatened by anything, I don't find much point in calling for rolls on extended actions (especially in a dream-centric chronicle that could require a billion such rolls). If the real meat of the story is in the dream, hurry up and get the PCs there!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oneiromancers who are very successful in their work are liable to leave deep impressions on the dreamer. A low-Intensity dream can be overwritten by something much more powerful, but the dreamer is likely to recall the details upon waking and with an exceptional success, the dreamer might even have an idea of who\u2019s responsible, whether they saw the oneiromancer or not (see specific bonuses and penalties below). </div> </div> </div>Yes, that mechanic was pretty counterintuitive. I get where the designers were coming from with the whole 'dreams are fragile' angle, but players tend to resent mechanics that punish their PCs for being good at something.<br /> <br /> I think the results of an exceptional success could be more clearly defined. Perhaps if the dreamer knows the oneiromancer personally, he recognizes her as the person who mucked around in his dream. Otherwise, he gets a bonus on rolls to find or identify her (Perception, Investigation, etc) equal to her Wyrd.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Changelings who dreamscape while sleeping in the Hedge or in a Hollow gain a +1 bonus. If the target dreamer is also sleeping in the Hedge or a Hollow, the bonus is +2, total. </div> </div> </div>I like the Hedge still being an attractive place for changelings to manipulate dreams from, since both are closely tied to the Wyrd. As some alternative food for thought, dreamscaping from the Hedge could provide a 'free' glamour point for use in every dream (or two if the target dreamer is also asleep). So while manipulating dreams from the Hedge is more convenient for changelings, it doesn't necessarily make them better at it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Analyze Dream</b> Intelligence + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity to recognize the type of dream. Storyteller rolls the Intensity and adds any successes to the player's results if the oneiromancer is analyzing a prophetic dream. </div> </div> </div>The last part is something we'll be trying out, going with the idea that true prophetic dreams stand out. </div> </div> </div>Definitely prefer this mechanic as an alternative to players saying &quot;we check to see if it's a prophetic dream before we do anything else, 'cause we don't wanna ruin it.&quot; Starting every dream sequence with a routine procedure is counter to the whimsical, surreal, and mysterious mood they should have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Search for Dream Poison:</b> Intelligence + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. Each roll takes all night and requires a number of total successes equal to the Intensity of the contagion-dream. Oneiromancers can use this technique to search for other types of intruders in dreams, making the same roll vs. the intruder\u2019s Wits or Manipulation + Stealth or Subterfuge + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity roll to hide or blend into the scene. Attacking an intruder leads to dream battle (see below) and is not handled through standard dreamscaping maneuvers. </div> </div> </div>I would have the roll be Dexterity + Stealth or Manipulation + Subterfuge. Physical Skills are typically paired with Physical Attributes, and they can still matter to dreamers by way of muscle memory.<br /> <br /> Otherwise, I like the change away from using Wits + Empathy for everything. It gives changelings who've invested in other Skills a chance to do more things in dreams.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sleep Teaching: Intelligence + appropriate mental or social Skill + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. The teacher must possess the Skill, Merit (Storyteller discretion), or Contract in question and cannot teach above their own ability level. The dreamer dreams about practicing skills in his sleep. One night of Dream Teaching is equivalent to a day of study. A critical success grants the dreamer a free experience point that can only be spent on the Skill, Merit, or Contract being taught. Can only have one point in storage per skill; once it is raised, another can be taught. </div> </div> </div>And just to update this to GMC rules, I'd have it award a Beat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Psychotherapy</b>: Composure + Empathy + Wyrd \u2013 Intensity. Each roll takes all night. Each night equals a week of normal psychotherapy. </div> </div> </div>I think this should be Presence + Empathy. Maybe Manipulation. Typically, power or finesse Attributes are used to actively accomplish tasks. From an in-universe perspective, a charismatic therapist is more likely to make an impression on his patients than a cool-headed one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In our games, if you sleep with a dream vessel and can dream, it has a chance to activate for you. You don't have to be fae in any sense of the word, which is part of why dream vessels are so very valuable. I wanted to have that spelled out for the future because as the group meets different types of creatures, no doubt it will come up. In the book, frustratingly, one sentence says that &quot;any dreamer may experience the dream,&quot; but the very next sentence restricts it to &quot;any changeling, fetch or human bound in a dreaming-tasked pledge.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Totally agreed. Letting non-fae use 'em opens up more storytelling possibilities. I could see a changeling planting dream vessels under his mortal family's pillows to turn them against his fetch, or under happier circumstances, weaving particular dreams for them upon request. (&quot;Daddy, can you have me fly tonight? Please?&quot; &quot;I wanna have dinosaurs in mine!&quot; D'aww.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I didn't feel the need to change much about dream battle; I just wanted to specify who is capable of actually engaging in proper dream combat, taking into account the creatures of later books. Non-lucid dreamers cannot be engaged in this special way, which requires the ability to respond in kind. They can play the stage for a dream battle between other lucid dreamers but cannot enter into it themselves. Not only does this make dream battle special, but it also prevents some abuses which could come up later on. It's nice to want to show up in a werewolf's dream and start beating the Willpower out of them, but changelings are not (yet) the True Fae and are not capable of that level of abuse. </div> </div> </div>I agree that changelings shouldn't be able to invade the dreams of other supernatural creatures willy-nilly, as said creatures don't have a universal way to retaliate in kind. That said, I think it might be more interesting to let changelings invade a supernatural foe's dreams in exchange for a nasty mental Condition (like Madness or Delusional), which affects them whether or not they win the dream combat. So if a motley is desperate for an edge against a potent foe, they can gamble for one, but there'll be a price tag attached.<br /> <br /> In general, I think it's more interesting for powerful options to carry hefty consequences than it is to take them off the board. Before I read Dancers in the Dusk, I liked the idea of letting changelings enter any mortal's dreams by spending extra glamour (DitD, of course, did something way cooler).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We have also gone on extended group adventures in a dreamscape, and I wanted some part of the rules to reflect this. The most recent example was when the party met in one motley member's dream and were guided along the Skein to the dream of a catatonic child none of them had had the chance to befriend or pledge with. They suspected that one of their Keepers had visited the girl and left her in such a state, and since the doctor's count help her, they had to try. Within her admittedly unique dreamscape, they found the parts of her psyche in disarray after a monster (a True Fae) had swept through and damaged her mental landscape. Her Courage, Memory, Safety, and Hope (all personified) were missing and the changelings went about trying to find them. They didn't use dream maneuvers as above but made general changes (to themselves) to navigate and to convince the aspects of her battered psyche to work with them, come back together, and try again. The one maneuver they finally made was changing the dream to bury the open wound left behind from when the Keeper rended into her dreams to read the Wyrd. They knew they'd gotten somewhere when she came to not long after. This is the kind of fun we've had through this sort of thing, so yes, I want to encourage it. </div> </div> </div>Oh wow, that's totally awesome. Pretty classic 'journey into the mind' trope to meet personifications of those kinds of concepts. Every story I've read/watched that used it was a delight to follow, so I can only imagine how much fun it was for your players to interact with in a game.<br /> <br /> Really, that's one of the things which is just so cool about dream sequences. Internal thoughts and feelings literally shape the scene, so they're pretty much the ultimate vehicle for character exploration and development.<br /> <br /> <br /> Thumbs up on all the other changes I didn't comment on! Once I get around to running a Changeling game I'd love to use these.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T15:03:14-07:00","id":155999,"title":"","content":"This stuff looks pretty damn good <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> If you want to play around with the Dream stuff in Dream Catchers be my guest <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T15:30:29-07:00","id":156029,"title":"","content":"Thank you for taking a look! I know there's a lot here and it's very mechanical. I'm looking forward to playtesting much of this stuff next weekend, and I'll have to look at Dream Catchers when I can! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Right now I am looking at refining and extending the Skeinwalking rules, and I came up with some advanced possibilities (that could also dovetail into a God-Machine Changeling Chronicle) this morning that really excited me!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T15:46:26-07:00","id":156040,"title":"","content":"False Ephiphany, I will have to get back to everything you mentioned. For now:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By this do you mean that changelings appear as their former human selves, or that they simply don't suffer their curse's penalties? (So wizened are less surly but still wrinkly-faced, ogres are less gullible but still brutish-looking, etc.) </div> </div> </div>The intention is that they are still fae and in the real world fully immersed in their seeming/mein, but in the dream world they get to shed the curse of their mein (so an Elemental would get 10 again Manpulation and so forth, finding the ability to express themselves in unique ways in the dreamscape). They can make their dream self look however they want; there's nothing I've found which says their dreaming reflection must reflect their mein.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And also cuts both ways, since their nightmares will be more likely to cost them Willpower. That fits with the general effects of having high dots in a power stat--extra benefits, but also extra drawbacks. (Though if you don't want that, they could also add their power stat to Willpower when resisting nightmares.) </div> </div> </div>I thought about that, but there's so much more than changelings can do in dreams (including Convince the Dreamer) and I went with the idea that supernatural dreams are overall more vivid/difficult to tamper with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the results of an exceptional success could be more clearly defined. Perhaps if the dreamer knows the oneiromancer personally, he recognizes her as the person who mucked around in his dream. Otherwise, he gets a bonus on rolls to find or identify her (Perception, Investigation, etc) equal to her Wyrd. </div> </div> </div>Ahhh, but I left them hazy on purpose. You ever met someone and felt something like deja vu, even if it couldn't have been? That irritable moment in which you don't trust them even if you don't know anything bad about them, or even know them at all? I was going to leave it as one way to explain such moments. It's not about identifying a face but sensing the intention/tampering in a visceral way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T12:57:35-07:00","id":156839,"title":"","content":"Some more thoughts (and some changes):<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As some alternative food for thought, dreamscaping from the Hedge could provide a 'free' glamour point for use in every dream (or two if the target dreamer is also asleep). So while manipulating dreams from the Hedge is more convenient for changelings, it doesn't necessarily make them better at it. </div> </div> </div>I was going with the idea not that it made them better at it, necessarily, but that the stuff of dreams is easier to manipulate when you're in a realm that's, in a way, closer to them. Everything you do is a little more effective. I suppose another mechanic would have been better to reflect that idea, such as granting 9-again. I'll have to chew on this one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I would have the roll be Dexterity + Stealth or Manipulation + Subterfuge. Physical Skills are typically paired with Physical Attributes, and they can still matter to dreamers by way of muscle memory. </div> </div> </div>The concept I'm going with for analysis maneuvers is that they are just that: analysis, the task of reviewing and interpreting data from the dream environment. You might show yourself physically searching the dream but your muscle memory is not what you're relying on for the job. What you're really doing is observing, taking apart the symbolism, and coming to a conclusion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And just to update this to GMC rules, I'd have it award a Beat. </div> </div> </div>Ah, thank you for pointing this out! I was trying to remind myself to update what I could for GMC compliance, but forgot that part. I've added it in.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think this should be Presence + Empathy. Maybe Manipulation. Typically, power or finesse Attributes are used to actively accomplish tasks. From an in-universe perspective, a charismatic therapist is more likely to make an impression on his patients than a cool-headed one. </div> </div> </div>I totally see what you mean here, and I've rethought my whole strategy. I was trying to incorporate some attributes that aren't used as often but that was the wrong motivation. Manipulation is what's used for psychotherapy (as per Asylum). Intelligence covers efforts to heal where you have the time to work on the patient (as per the core). Yeah, I've reworked the rolls in that category, as well as tweaking one or two others. The spread is now 4 Intelligence-based maneuvers, 3 Wits based, 4 Manipulation based, and 1 Composure-based (as per the Changeling core about soaking up emotions in the ether at an emotionally charged event). <br /> <br /> This is just the sort of oversight I knew I needed. I'm not done going over all the points you made yet, but thank you! <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I could see a changeling planting dream vessels under his mortal family's pillows to turn them against his fetch, or under happier circumstances, weaving particular dreams for them upon request. (&quot;Daddy, can you have me fly tonight? Please?&quot; &quot;I wanna have dinosaurs in mine!&quot; D'aww.) </div> </div> </div>D'aww indeed! That's adorable!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-03T20:45:12-07:00","id":158630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KismetRose</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ahhh, but I left them hazy on purpose. You ever met someone and felt something like deja vu, even if it couldn't have been? That irritable moment in which you don't trust them even if you don't know anything bad about them, or even know them at all? I was going to leave it as one way to explain such moments. It's not about identifying a face but sensing the intention/tampering in a visceral way.</div> </div> </div> </div> Ohh, I see. That seems fitting for meeting someone in a dream to leave only dream-like impressions, vague feelings and hazy memories rather than a clear face. I think you could still model that concept with a + [target's Wyrd] bonus on rolls to find or identify the oneiromancer, as I can picture it being easier for someone to spot her in a crowd (Perception) or have a nagging feeling when she's lying (Wits + Subterfuge) after she's left a deep impression in their dreams. The ST can drop other hints and portents (as ever); I'd just find it useful to have some defined mechanics as a starting point.<br /> <br /> Mechanics can also be a great way to convey mystery to players. &quot;I roll Perception.&quot; &quot;Okay, do it at +3.&quot; &quot;Sweet! Where's the bonus from?&quot; &quot;You don't know, but something seems to be helping you out.&quot; Old-fashioned descriptions can do it too, of course, but unexplained bonuses/penalties raise a way bigger question mark (since they have direct and immediate impact on PCs).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I was going with the idea not that it made them better at it, necessarily, but that the stuff of dreams is easier to manipulate when you're in a realm that's, in a way, closer to them. Everything you do is a little more effective. I suppose another mechanic would have been better to reflect that idea, such as granting 9-again. I'll have to chew on this one. </div> </div> </div> I like 9-again more than the glamour point, as it has precedent in existing mechanics and makes the roll more likely to do better without providing a flat bonus. Another possibility is for three successes to count as an exceptional success rather than five. (Blood &amp; Smoke used that for a few of its vampire Conditions.)<br /> <br /> For the record, though, I think a flat bonus is usually a fine way to reflect something being better. My line of thinking was that if being in a location nets the bonus, it'll encourage players to always go there (and have the same net effect as the &quot;you can only use oneiromancy in the Hedge&quot; rule, only with PCs more likely to succeed at rolls). Then again, depending on how hazardous past trips to the Hedge have been, they might figure a +1 isn't always worth it...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The concept I'm going with for analysis maneuvers is that they are just that: analysis, the task of reviewing and interpreting data from the dream environment. You might show yourself physically searching the dream but your muscle memory is not what you're relying on for the job. What you're really doing is observing, taking apart the symbolism, and coming to a conclusion. </div> </div> </div> Whoopsies, I think the focus of my comment was unclear; I meant intruders trying to hide within the dream. I can see that using the normal ol' Dexterity + Stealth (if you can hide in the real world, your skill carries over) or Manipulation + Subterfuge (because dreamscapes are metaphysical, and respond to attempts to manipulate them on that same level).<br /> <br /> Glad the rest has been helpful!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-04T12:44:43-07:00","id":159289,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think you could still model that concept with a + [target's Wyrd] bonus on rolls to find or identify the oneiromancer, as I can picture it being easier for someone to spot her in a crowd (Perception) or have a nagging feeling when she's lying (Wits + Subterfuge) after she's left a deep impression in their dreams. The ST can drop other hints and portents (as ever); I'd just find it useful to have some defined mechanics as a starting point. </div> </div> </div>The concept is that the effect isn't something the target actively controls or even probably realizes is happening; it's largely subconscious and will often be something that goes unexplained. <br /> <br /> I think I'd rather give the target an Intelligence + Composure roll to even realize that there's a deeper reason for their reaction to the changeling. The target would need to meet or exceed the changeling's roll on the dream maneuver to tie the feeling to the changeling directly; an exceptional success would bring the full memory of the dream back and bring certainty that the changeling is responsible, even if they can't explain how. No successes would mean that they find no real reason for the reaction.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I like 9-again more than the glamour point, as it has precedent in existing mechanics and makes the roll more likely to do better without providing a flat bonus. Another possibility is for three successes to count as an exceptional success rather than five. (Blood &amp; Smoke used that for a few of its vampire Conditions.)<br /> <br /> For the record, though, I think a flat bonus is usually a fine way to reflect something being better. My line of thinking was that if being in a location nets the bonus, it'll encourage players to always go there (and have the same net effect as the &quot;you can only use oneiromancy in the Hedge&quot; rule, only with PCs more likely to succeed at rolls). Then again, depending on how hazardous past trips to the Hedge have been, they might figure a +1 isn't always worth it... </div> </div> </div>I'm still chewing over this one. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Glad the rest has been helpful! </div> </div> </div>All of it has been helpful, because you've made me look deeper into it and question my motives and methods. Thank you again! =)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}